    "narrative": "The Austin Waves lineup for May 27th is anchored by **Andy Pages**, who earns our highest efficiency score of 73.98. His matchup against Sugano is opportunistic; the opposing starter\u2019s 4.79 xERA provides the softest landing spot on the card, and Sugano\u2019s slow delivery offers a rare chance to exploit **Andy Pages**' speed on the basepaths, which is critical given our aggressive pursuit of the scarcest category: stolen bases. Close behind are **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** (67.55) and **Gunnar Henderson** (63.72). **Gunnar Henderson** faces a tough lefty in Matz, but his massive +16% park factor adjustment overrides the platoon disadvantage, making him a mandatory start as he anchors our heart of the order.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench today was **Miguel Vargas**, who finished just a fraction of a point behind **Josh Jung** (52.63 vs 52.62) for the final UTIL slot. While **Miguel Vargas** draws a decent matchup against Prielipp, the algorithm favored the reliability of **Josh Jung** batting in the cleanup spot. Conversely, **Jackson Merrill** saw his efficiency plummet to 38.94 due to a brutal combination: facing the elite arm of S\u00e1nchez (2.88 xERA) while hindered by a high-velocity catcher exchange, making him a bench risk to preserve our game caps.\n\nWe are proceeding with caution regarding **Miguel Andujar**, whose score of 48.82 is buoyed by a platoon edge but threatened by the 2.88 xERA of S\u00e1nchez. While we need his production, he represents our lowest-floor starter today. Meanwhile, we continue to monitor the status of **Jordan Westburg**, who remains sidelined on the IL. As we navigate the schedule, the goal remains clear: prioritize the high-sprint-speed profiles like **Andy Pages** to manufacture SBs, even when facing tougher arms like Gausman, who represents a moderate challenge for **Liam Hicks** (50.36) in the early slot.",
